# Copy to .env.local. Both values are inlined into the client bundle at build
# time, so whichever project you point at is the one that build talks to —
# permanently, for that artifact.
#
# POINT LOCAL DEVELOPMENT AT DEV, NEVER AT PRODUCTION.
#
#   letsride-dev  DEV. Use this one. Not created yet — see docs/ENVIRONMENTS.md
#   letsride      PRODUCTION, ref zwprydcyryvudhurbnye. Real riders. Not for local work.
#
# The key is the *publishable* key — Supabase MCP `get_publishable_keys`, or the
# dashboard. It ships in the bundle by design. The service-role key must never
# appear here; src/__tests__/no-service-role-key.test.ts is the tripwire.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-dev-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-publishable-key-here

# Only needed for a NATIVE build (`CAPACITOR_BUILD=1`), where it is REQUIRED and
# next.config.ts fails the build without it. Leave it unset for the web app and
# for local development: src/lib/origin.ts then falls back to
# window.location.origin, which is what makes every Vercel host — production,
# DEV and per-deployment preview aliases — keep working with no configuration.
#
# It is the origin for URLs that LEAVE the app: shared postcard links, and the
# signup-confirmation and password-recovery links GoTrue emails. Inside the
# shell the runtime origin is https://localhost, which is on no redirect
# allowlist, and an unlisted redirect_to is discarded silently rather than
# refused. A release bundle uses the production host:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CANONICAL_ORIGIN=https://app.letsride.social
#
# Leave it UNSET here and in every Vercel target. A web build refuses it rather
# than tolerating it, because set on Preview it would have DEV and feature
# branches email confirmation links pointing at production, where the token is
# invalid. It is for `npm run build:native` only.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CANONICAL_ORIGIN=


# ---- Error reporting (PD-315) -------------------------------------------
# Sentry's DSN. Public by design — it ships in the client bundle, like the
# Supabase publishable key above; it authorises writing events and nothing else.
#
# UNSET IS A CLEAN NO-OP and is the normal state locally. Nothing throws, no
# console line is printed, and `npm run walk` is unaffected — so leave it unset
# unless you are deliberately testing the transport.
#
# One Sentry project, split by the environment tag rather than by project —
# filtering an environment out of an issue list is one click. That is the
# opposite of the PostHog answer below, and deliberately: a PostHog funnel
# aggregates its whole project by default, so there a DEV event corrupts a
# PROD number silently.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=development

# ---- Product analytics (PD-353) -----------------------------------------
# PostHog's project API key. Public by design — it ships in the client bundle.
#
# PRODUCTION SCOPE ONLY, and leave it unset everywhere else including here. The
# free tier allows ONE project, and a PostHog project is the analytics boundary:
# a funnel aggregates everything in it by default, so a DEV event corrupts a
# PROD number silently unless every insight remembers to filter it out. The
# opposite of the Sentry answer above, and deliberately so.
#
# The cost of that is real and named in src/lib/analytics/client.ts: nothing
# outside production can reach this, so `npm run walk` cannot exercise it and
# the transport gets one hand-verification on PROD. Unset is a clean no-op.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://eu.i.posthog.com
